Original commit body from 330014b721bf3b7e7baa4a24e29e3166acef0244:

test_validation_report_empty.py failed on all four substrates with 2
un-allow-listed ADR-0023 §3 runtime_coherence offenders against a
shrink-only baseline of 1, plus a stale go route assertion. Both were
invisible because smart-test selects that file only when a module it
imports changes, and dev's committed manifest did not contain it — so
every merged PR passed CI over a red gate (INV-kinin's first measured
consequence).

Provenance measured, not inferred: at 9baaa9afbd^ (the parent of
WI-duguk's "emit enum variants and trait members") the corpus yields
exactly 1 offender. WI-duguk introduced the second by rooting a trait
over its own required methods.

Ruled LEGITIMATE and allow-listed via an ADR-0023 §3 amendment. The two
edge types do not share a destination — contains names the trait's own
requirement (MyTrait::method), dispatches_to names the implementation
(MyStruct::method) — so this is two distinct relationships over one
endpoint shape, the same reasoning that admitted the file-anchor
entries. The baseline was NOT raised: a shrink-only baseline lifted to
accept a breach ratifies the drift.

The entry rules on the partition only and explicitly does not ratify the
anchoring that made it necessary. Root cause is filed as INV-tihim:
linkers/type_hierarchy is the framework-agnostic linker that emits
interface-method -> impl-method dispatches_to, and it cannot fire for
Rust because its name-splitting knows "#" and "." but not "::" — while
linkers/containment's _SEPARATORS knows all three. One fact, two homes.
Measured on the corpus, every other language already anchors dispatch
method->method; Rust is the sole outlier.

Two stale rationales removed rather than worked around: the linker
docstring's "the Rust analyzer does not emit the trait's required-method
list" (false since WI-duguk), and the in-test claim that amending the
allow-list would be "governance-touching" (neither docs/adr/** nor the
allowlist yaml is on the AGENTS.md governance list) — that sentence is
what made the breach look unfixable in-band.

Go fix: route markers moved to "{METHOD} {path}" with handler identity
in meta (WI-zugob). The assertion now reads meta["handler_name"] —
measured; the item proposed meta["handler_ref"], which other producers
use but the go path does not emit.

